From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AML: start on a keyframe after the decoder resets itself

When a packet is cut short in the decoder, the decoder is reset and the
packet fed again. Only the decoder was reset:
CDVDVideoCodecAmlogic::Reset runs on a seek alone, so the bitstream
converter's keyframe wait, the bit parser's m_has_keyframe, the
frame_num rewriter, the MVC parameter wait and the seek preroll all
carried on as if the stream were continuous. The reset decoder then
started on whatever picture came next, usually not a keyframe, and
showed broken pictures until the next one (on MVC, in both views).

The wrapper now runs ResetStream(), the part of Reset() that is about
the decoder and the stream, the seek preroll included; the player's drop
request is set again at the start of the next AddData. The packet is
then fed again from the demuxer payload, through the gate below and the
reset converter. The decoder timeout is the same case: CAMLCodec's
GetPicture returns VC_FLUSHED, the player resets the codec and feeds
nothing again, so the stream goes on mid-GOP. A Reset() that follows a
VC_FLUSHED is handled like a self-reset; a seek is not.

H.264 and HEVC then drop packets up to one decoding can start on, read
from the demuxer payload before anything else runs on it: on HEVC an
IRAP picture (NAL types 16 to 21); on H.264 an IDR picture, a recovery
point SEI, or an I slice first in an access unit that carries an SPS,
for streams that have neither. The converter's and the bit parser's own
waits also open on a lone SPS, which is enough after a seek, which
lands on a keyframe, but not here. The gate opens on such a packet, or
after ten seconds of pictures without one, so a stream without
keyframes still plays. On MVC the parameter set wait follows the gate,
so the two hold a restart for at most about twelve seconds. A packet
the gate drops also ends the count of resets for the packet before it.

The gate comes before the full buffer check, so while it is closed
nothing has gone into the reset decoder and nothing can be refused.
Until a packet passes it GetPicture reports that it needs data without
asking the decoder: the decoder timeout would otherwise flush the codec
on a stream whose keyframes are farther apart than the timeout. From
the packet that opens it on, the decoder is asked, so one that refuses
or stalls on it is still reset by the timeout, which restarts when the
gate opens. Before then GetPicture reports the end of a drain only when
the player asked for the drain after the gate closed; a drain flag left
over from before, as after a timeout during a still frame drain, would
otherwise end the drain at once and let the player start early. Other
codecs keep what a seek gives them, as before.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

